---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import SectionHeading from '../components/SectionHeading.astro';
import { SITE, IDENTITY } from '../lib/site';

const capabilities = [
  {
    title: 'Product & Web Platforms',
    body: 'Marketing sites, portals, dashboards and operational web applications built as coherent systems.',
    tech: 'Astro · TypeScript · Supabase · Vercel',
  },
  {
    title: 'ERP & Operations',
    body: 'Internal systems that model real factory workflows, stock movement, traceability and order progress.',
    tech: 'ERPNext · Frappe · Python · PostgreSQL',
  },
  {
    title: 'AI & Automation',
    body: 'AI-assisted workflows, integrations, provider routing, document analysis and operational automation where they provide practical value.',
    tech: 'Provider routing · Vision analysis · Zod',
  },
  {
    title: 'Ecommerce & Digital Experience',
    body: 'Production storefronts, catalogue architecture, responsive product experience and commerce implementation.',
    tech: 'Shopify · Catalogue design · Responsive',
  },
];
---

<Layout
  title="About"
  description="Hamza Ali builds software from inside the operations it supports: web platforms, manufacturing ERP and production storefronts for real businesses."
  path="/about"
>
  <section class="border-b border-border">
    <div class="container-site pt-14 pb-12 sm:pt-20 sm:pb-16">
      <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'About' }]} />
      <p class="mt-8 font-mono text-[0.7rem] uppercase tracking-[0.22em] text-accent">
        {SITE.author} · {SITE.role}
      </p>
      <h1 class="mt-4 max-w-2xl font-display text-4xl font-normal tracking-[-0.01em] text-text-primary sm:text-5xl">
        I build software where the work happens
      </h1>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container-site py-14 sm:py-20">
    <div class="prose-case">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
        Working inside a garment manufacturing business gave me direct exposure to the problems
        software is supposed to solve: quote intake, stock movement, order tracking, client
        communication and production visibility.
      </p>
      <p>
        That shaped how I build. I prefer systems that survive real workflows over prototypes
        designed only to look impressive. When a quote form can fail quietly, or a stock record
        disagrees with the shelf, you learn quickly which engineering decisions actually matter.
      </p>

      <h2>What I do now</h2>
      <p>
        I lead IT and digital systems at Reve Stitching, a knitted-garment manufacturer in
        Faisalabad. That means two connected bodies of work: the customer-facing digital
        platform, covering the website, quote and sample intake, admin operations and a client
        portal, and the internal side, an ERPNext implementation customized to how the factory
        actually moves material, from batch tracking to gate passes and operational reporting.
      </p>
      <p>
        Alongside that I build products of my own. Aurelia, a community management platform for
        Discord, is the largest: a Python service with a web dashboard, public documentation and
        a multi-provider AI layer. I also carried Haus Couture, a UK streetwear label, from a
        custom storefront onto its production Shopify store.
      </p>

      <h2>How I got here</h2>
      <p>
        I'm self-taught. The path ran through real work rather than coursework: a business that
        needed a website, then needed it to do more, then needed its operations digitized, then
        needed the two sides to talk to each other. Each step demanded more than the last, and
        the option was always to learn it or to stop. I learned it.
      </p>
      <p>
        That origin shows in how I work. I default to proximity: watch the real workflow before
        designing the system, sit with the person who will use the screen, and treat maintenance
        as part of the job rather than an afterthought. Software I build is running in
        production today because it was built with the people who run it.
      </p>

      <h2>How I work</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Systems over screens.</strong> A page is easy; the pipeline behind it, intake,
          validation, storage, reporting, integration, is the actual product.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Fail-safe ordering.</strong> Record the submission first, sync everything else
          after. A downstream outage should degrade a system, not break it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Drafts, not verdicts.</strong> Where AI participates, it prepares work for a
          human to confirm. It never makes the commercial decision.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Plain-language engineering.</strong> The system should be explainable to the
          person using it, in terms they already understand from their own workflow.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        The fastest way to reach me is email at
        <a href={`mailto:${IDENTITY.email}`}>{IDENTITY.email}</a>, or through the
        <a href="/contact">contact page</a>. I'm based in {SITE.location} and currently
        {SITE.availability.toLowerCase()}.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="border-t border-border bg-midnight">
    <div class="container-site py-14 sm:py-20">
      <SectionHeading eyebrow="Capabilities" title="What I build" />
      <div class="mt-8 grid gap-px overflow-hidden rounded-card border border-border bg-border sm:grid-cols-2">
        {
          capabilities.map((cap) => (
            <div class="bg-midnight p-6 sm:p-8">
              <h3 class="text-base font-semibold tracking-tight text-text-primary">{cap.title}</h3>
              <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">{cap.body}</p>
              <p class="mt-4 font-mono text-[0.68rem] text-text-muted">{cap.tech}</p>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
